''chion''; < ME. ''trunchon'', ''trunchone'', ''trunchyne'', ''tronchon'', ''tronchoun'', < OF. ''tronçon'', ''tronson'', a truncheon, a thick slice, a piece cut off, F. ''tronçon'' (= Pr. ''tronso'', ''troncho'', ''trenson'' = Sp. ''troncon'' = It. ''troncone''), dim. of ''tronc'', a stump, trunk: see ''trunk''.] '''1.''' A trunk, stock, or stump, as of a tree; hence, a tree the branches of which have been lopped off to produce rapid growth.
{{smaller block|And tho bowis grewen out of stockis or ''tronchons'', and the ''tronchons'' or schaftis grewen out of the roote.
{{right|''Bp. Pecock'', Repressor, i. 6.}}}}
'''2.''' The shaft of a spear or lance.
{{smaller block|He foyneth on his feet with his ''tronchoun''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Knight’s Tale, l. 1757.}}
They carry also the ''truncheons'' of their Lances with their Standards and Ensignes trailing along the ground.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 304.}}}}
'''3.''' A short staff; a club; a cudgel. ''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 504.
{{smaller block|One with a broken ''truncheon'' deals his blows.
{{right|''Dryden'', Pal. and Arc., iii. 612.}}}}
'''4.''' A baton or staff of authority; specifically, in ''her.'', the staff of the earl marshal of England. {{smaller|Two of these truncheons are borne saltierwise behind the escutcheon of the Duke of Norfolk, who is hereditary earl marshal. See ''marshal’s staff'', under ''marshal''<sup>1</sup>.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Well, believe this, . . .
The marshal’s ''truncheon'', nor the judge’s robe,
Become them with one half so good a grace
As mercy does. {{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., ii. 2. 61.}}</poem>
No sooner are the Three Strokes given, but out jumps Four ''Trunchion'' Officers from their Hovel, and with a sort of ill mannerly Reverence receive him at the Grate.
{{right|Quoted in ''Ashton’s'' Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne, [II. 243.}}}}

'''truncheon''' (trun′chon), ''v. t.'' [< ''truncheon'', ''n.'']

