'''trundle''' (trun′dl), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''trundled'', ppr. ''trundling''. [< OF. ''trondeler'', trundle; ult. a var. of ''trendle'', ''trindle''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To roll, as something on low wheels or casters; move or bowl along, as a round body; hence, to move with a rolling gait.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Betty.'' They are gone, sir, in great Anger.
''Petulant.'' Enough, let ’em ''trundle.''</poem>
{{right|''Congreve'', Way of the World, i. 9.}}
<poem>Fast our goodman ''trundled'' down the hill.</poem>
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, II. 203.}}
The four horses . . . seemed dwarfed by the blundering structure which ''trundled'' at their heels.
{{right|''J. Hawthorne'', Dust, p. 11.}}}}
'''2.''' To revolve; twirl.
{{smaller block|<poem>And there he threw the wash about,
On both sides of the way,
Just like unto a ''trundling'' mop.</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', John Gilpin.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To roll, or cause to roll, as a circular or spherical thing or as something on casters or low wheels: as, to ''trundle'' a hoop; to ''trundle'' a wheelbarrow; hence, to cause to move off with a rolling gait or pace.
{{smaller block|<poem>She took an apple out of her pocket,
And ''trundled'' it along the plain.</poem>
{{right|''Sir Hugh'' (Child’s Ballads, III. 335).}}
They . . . who play at nine holes, and who ''trundle'' little round stones.
{{right|''Holland'', tr. of Plutarch, p. 1089.}}
I’ll clap a pair of horses to your chaise that shall ''trundle'' you off in a twinkling.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', She Stoops to Conquer, ii.}}
''Trundling'' the hoop is a pastime of uncertain origin.
{{right|''Strutt'', Sports and Pastimes, p. 490.}}}}
'''2.''' To cause to revolve; twirl: as, to ''trundle'' a

